{"builder":"Goal: when the config JSON is malformed, the app must still stop with exit 1 and a `bad config:` line on stderr, and that line must also give the file path and the line and column of the mistake. Do NOT adopt the fix suggested in the request (catching the error in `loadConfig` and returning `{}` or the defaults). That would hide the typo and break the README's documented `bad config: <message>` and exit 1.\n\nCurrent code: `src/config.js` `loadConfig(path)` returns `JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`. `src/app.js` catches any error, writes `bad config: ${error.message}` to stderr and exits 1, and otherwise writes `listening on <port>` to stdout. `test/config.test.js` has two tests: 'reads a config' and 'bad JSON throws', which asserts `SyntaxError`. `npm test` runs `node --test`. The repo's Node is v22.\n\nSteps:\n1. In `src/config.js`, read the file, then call `JSON.parse` inside a try/catch. On a parse error, throw again. Do not return anything. The new error's `message` must contain `path` exactly as it was passed in (do not resolve it to an absolute path) and the 1-based line and column of the mistake, e.g. `<path>: line 3 column 3: <original message>` or `<path>:3:3: ...`. Compute line and column yourself so they do not depend on the Node version's message format: take the character position from the original message (`/position (\\d+)/`) and count newlines in the text up to that offset. If Node's message has no position, fall back to its `(line N column M)` if present. Prefer throwing a `SyntaxError` (or setting `cause` to the original error) so the existing 'bad JSON throws' test still passes. If you change the error type, update that test so `npm test` still passes. Keep read errors such as ENOENT as they are: they still get thrown and still lead to the exit-1 `bad config:` line.\n2. Leave the behaviour of `src/app.js` unchanged: `bad config: ${error.message}` goes to stderr, then exit 1, and nothing goes to stdout on failure. Valid configs must load exactly as before. `{\"port\": 9000}` must give `listening on 9000` with exit 0, and `loadConfig` must return `{ port: 9000 }`.\n3. Add tests in `test/config.test.js` that would fail on the current code. (a) `loadConfig` on a temp file containing `{port: 9000` throws, and the error's message includes that file's path. (b) For a file with the four lines `{`, `  \"port\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \"x\"`, `}`, the message includes the path, line 3 and column 3. If you like, you can also add a test that spawns `node src/app.js <file>` and checks for exit 1, empty stdout and the stderr content.\n4. Check your work by hand: d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > $d/c.json; node src/app.js $d/c.json; echo $?. Expect exit 1, no stdout, and stderr with `bad config:`, `$d/c.json`, line 3 and column 3. Also run the ok.json case and `npm test`.\n5. You may update the README line to mention the file path and location, but this is optional.\n\nOut of scope: swallowing parse errors or returning defaults, changing how a missing file is reported (beyond keeping it an exit-1 `bad config:` failure), validating config values, and supporting comments or JSON5.","verifier":"Run every check at the PR head on Node v22 (`node --version`). Use a fresh temp dir per check.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/c.json`, where c.json contains the four lines `{`, `  \\\"port\\\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \\\"x\\\"`, `}`, exits with code 1 and prints nothing on stdout (in particular no `listening on` line).\"\nRun: d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > $d/c.json; node src/app.js $d/c.json >$d/out 2>$d/err; echo \"exit $?\"; cat $d/out\nPASS: the exit code is 1 and $d/out is empty. FAIL: any other exit code, or any stdout output (such as `listening on ...`).\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"In that same run, stderr contains `bad config:`, the full path `<tmp>/c.json` exactly as passed on the command line, the line number `3` and the column number `3` of the mistake.\"\nRun: the same setup as Check 1, then cat $d/err.\nPASS: stderr contains `bad config:`, contains the exact string \"$d/c.json\" as passed, and gives line 3 and column 3 of the mistake in any clear form (e.g. `line 3 column 3`, `3:3`, `line 3, col 3`). FAIL: any of these is missing, the path is altered, or the line or column is not 3.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"Calling `loadConfig(path)` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws instead of returning a value, and the thrown error's `message` contains `path`.\"\nRun: d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > $d/b.json; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; try { const v = loadConfig('$d/b.json'); console.log('RETURNED', JSON.stringify(v)); process.exit(2) } catch (e) { console.log(e.message.includes('$d/b.json') ? 'OK' : 'NO PATH: ' + e.message) }\"\nPASS: prints `OK`. FAIL: prints `RETURNED ...` or `NO PATH: ...`.\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/ok.json`, where ok.json contains `{\\\"port\\\": 9000}`, exits 0 and prints `listening on 9000` on stdout. `loadConfig` on that file returns `{ port: 9000 }`.\"\nRun: d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > $d/ok.json; node src/app.js $d/ok.json; echo \"exit $?\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; console.log(JSON.stringify(loadConfig('$d/ok.json')))\"\nPASS: stdout shows `listening on 9000`, the exit code is 0, and the second command prints `{\"port\":9000}`. FAIL: anything else.\n\nCheck 5. Expect line: \"`npm test` passes, and the test suite has at least one test that fails at the current base commit and checks that the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path.\"\nRun: `npm test` at the head. Then, in a scratch copy or worktree, keep the head's test/ files, restore the base commit's src/ (git checkout <base> -- src), and run `npm test` again. Restore src/ afterwards.\nPASS: `npm test` passes at the